UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 39, Lesson 3: Fair and unfair games', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Fair games give equally likely outcomes for players or colours when that is the design; unfair games bias some outcomes.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nIs a cube labelled 1,1,1,2,3,4 fair for rolling a 1 versus a 2? Explain.\n\nFEEDBACK\nUnfair for 1 vs 2 — three faces show 1 and only one shows 2.\n\nWATCH FOR\nCalling a game fair because “anyone can win” ignores unequal outcome counts.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nJudge ten games fair/unfair with outcome reasons. Redesign two unfair games to be fairer.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Fair games give equally likely outcomes for players or colours when that is the design; unfair games bias some outcomes.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Problem solving","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-CHAN-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Judge ten games fair/unfair with outcome reasons. Redesign two unfair games to be fairer. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for fair; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Calling a game fair because “anyone can win” ignores unequal outcome counts.. Correction uses Unfair for 1 vs 2 — three faces show 1 and only one shows 2.. Why: restores accurate fair.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Calling a game fair because “anyone can win” ignores unequal outcome counts. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses fair with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with fair, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for fair, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers. / Accept a named misconception about fair, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for fair. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Unfair for 1 vs 2 — three faces show 1 and only one shows 2","Calling a game fair because “anyone can win” ignores unequal outcome counts","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson"],"question":"Is a cube labelled 1,1,1,2,3,4 fair for rolling a 1 versus a 2? Explain. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for fair; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Fair games give equally likely outcomes for players or colours when that is the design","Unfair for 1 vs 2 — three faces show 1 and only one shows 2","Careful use of unfair and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Calling a game fair because “anyone can win” ignores unequal outcome counts"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with fair?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named unfair and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Calling a game fair because “anyone can win” ignores unequal outcome counts","Name unfair and explain why it matters only, without linking it to fair","Apply fair to new material and name the unfair and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies fair with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value, quantity or the asked operation. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change digits to look neater without checking place value","Calling a game fair because “anyone can win” ignores unequal outcome counts","Show each place, value or step and say why that step is valid","Write only a final number with no working"],"question":"Which working best supports a correct answer for this lesson on fair?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the taught fair method; the others break place value, units or the asked quantity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore units and treat 1,1,1,2,3,4 and 1 as interchangeable","Unfair for 1 vs 2 — three faces show 1 and only one shows 2","Calling a game fair because “anyone can win” ignores unequal outcome counts","Read 1,1,1,2,3,4 from left to right as ones only, ignoring place value"],"question":"Using the method for fair, which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus, not by blending answers. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Average the two answers without checking either","Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed","Pick the longer-looking working automatically","Delete units so the answers appear to match"],"question":"If two methods disagree on a fair problem with numbers such as 1,1,1,2,3,4 and 1, what should you check first?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same mathematical method and a named reason. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Calling a game fair because “anyone can win” ignores unequal outcome counts","Use the same fair method on new numbers and name the places or values that matter","Retype the worked model numbers 1,1,1,2,3,4 and 1 as the answer","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers"],"question":"Solve a fresh fair problem that uses different numbers from the worked model and explain one key step.?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Recognising the warned misconception protects accuracy on new problems. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Unfair for 1 vs 2 — three faces show 1 and only one shows 2","Label units when comparing 1,1,1,2,3,4 with 1","Calling a game fair because “anyone can win” ignores unequal outcome counts","Fair games give equally likely outcomes for players or colours when that is the design"],"question":"Which error is the lesson warning against for fair?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for fair, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses fair with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for fair; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a named misconception about fair, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with fair, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Calling a game fair because “anyone can win” ignores unequal outcome counts.. Correction uses Unfair for 1 vs 2 — three faces show 1 and only one shows 2.. Why: restores accurate fair.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y4-w39-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise fair.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Statistics and Probability","success_criteria":["I can use fair with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify unfair and explain why it matters.","I can practise game independently on a new prompt.","I can notice outcome needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Fair games give equally likely outcomes for players or colours when that is the design; unfair games bias some outcomes.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working","Fair games give equally likely outcomes for players or colours when that is the design"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about fair with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nFair: coin with two faces if both count. Unfair: spinner with three-quarters red vs one-quarter blue if claiming even chance. Explain using outcomes.\n\nTRY FIRST\nIs a cube labelled 1,1,1,2,3,4 fair for rolling a 1 versus a 2? Explain.\n\nFEEDBACK\nUnfair for 1 vs 2 — three faces show 1 and only one shows 2.","explain":"Fair games give equally likely outcomes for players or colours when that is the design; unfair games bias some outcomes.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Calling a game fair because “anyone can win” ignores unequal outcome counts. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Fair and unfair games.","caption":"Count outcomes.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Fair</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">equal outcomes</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Unfair</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">biased faces</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Fair: coin with two faces if both count. Unfair: spinner with three-quarters red vs one-quarter blue if claiming even ch Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Fair: coin with two faces if both count. Unfair: spinner with three-quarters red vs one-quarter blue if claiming even ch Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about fair with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nFair: coin with two faces if both count. Unfair: spinner with three-quarters red vs one-quarter blue if claiming even chance. Explain using outcomes. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Outcome fairness vs slogan.","caption":"Ten judgements.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Three 1s</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">unfair vs 2</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">anyone can win = fair</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about fair with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCalling a game fair because “anyone can win” ignores unequal outcome counts. Here is a more productive model: Fair: coin with two faces if both count. Unfair: spinner with three-quarters red vs one-quarter blue if claiming even chance. Explain using outcomes.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\nUnfair for 1 vs 2 — three faces show 1 and only one shows 2.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Calling a game fair because “anyone can win” ignores unequal outcome counts. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working","Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about fair with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nFair games give equally likely outcomes for players or colours when that is the design; unfair games bias some outcomes. Now choose different numbers to show you can transfer the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve one fresh problem tha